gm-method (a user re-reports the SAME visual symptom after a prior fix -- 'X only shows parts' -- do NOT assume the prior fix regressed, and do NOT assume it's a new bug; WITNESS the live current state first, then prove complete-vs-incomplete by a force-draw A/B + a screenshot): a model looking 'fragmented / only parts visible' has a finite set of causes, each eliminable by one live measurement: (1) buried in terrain -> compare worldMinY vs groundHeight at the footprint; (2) culled -> monkeypatch the per-cluster/per-instance cull to force-draw everything and compare drawn-tris% AND a screenshot -- if force-draw adds NO new visible STRUCTURE (only more triangles on already-visible surfaces), the cull is LOD-correct and is NOT the cause; (3) bake dropped geometry -> diff the rendered mesh/prim count vs the source AND the user's original GLB (equal counts = nothing dropped); (4) material/normal/lighting -> dump material.type/color/side + scene lights + sample geometry normals (healthy MeshStandardMaterial + non-degenerate normals + a real directional sun = not a shading bug). When ALL eliminate and the geometry is provably complete, the 'only parts' is the model's GENUINE sparse geometry (e.g. a CS-style aim arena is a deck + a couple of walls -- it READS as fragments but IS the model), an expectation mismatch no code fixes. SURFACE that to the user as a decision (AskUserQuestion: 'it is complete / a specific part is missing / re-convert / it is the lighting') rather than inventing a fix -- the honest deliverable is the measurement + verdict. A force-draw screenshot that looks identical to the culled one is the single clearest proof that 'missing parts' are not being hidden.